  "plan": {
    "goal": "Perfused liver-on-chip metabolism study with antipyrine (low-extraction probe) to measure intrinsic hepatic metabolic capacity.",
    "rationale": "Antipyrine is a low-extraction (E < 0.3) capacity-limited probe reflecting intrinsic enzyme activity (Rowland & Tozer). We target E = 0.15 (inlet 10 µM, outlet 8.5 µM), squarely in the low-extraction regime, so clearance Cl = E·Q reflects intrinsic metabolic capacity rather than flow. At 2 µL/min perfusion, Cl = 0.15 × 2 = 0.30 µL/min. Primary human hepatocytes (PHH) are the gold-standard liver cell (BSL-2, do not divide in vitro), seeded at 150,000 cells/cm² on a 0.08 cm² channel (12,000 cells). The 400×100 µm channel at 2 µL/min gives 0.05 Pa wall shear (low end of hepatic sinusoidal 0.05–0.15 Pa), Re = 0.13 (laminar), residence time ~24 s. O2 supply/demand ratio ≈ 14 (not hypoxic). Recirculating system volume 200 µL gives t½ ≈ 7.7 h, consistent with slow antipyrine clearance.",
